{"name":"Estuary Blue","hex":"#70a5b7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"estuary","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A coastal water body where ocean tides and river water merge, resulting in a brackish water zone.","An ocean inlet also fed by fresh river water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/estuary"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Estuary","blocks":[{"text":"An estuary is a partially enclosed coastal body of brackish water where freshwater from rivers or streams meets and mixes with saltwater from the open sea. Estuaries form transition zones between riverine and marine environments and are classified as ecotones, areas where different ecosystems overlap. They are influenced by both marine processes and fluvial processes. The mixing of seawater and freshwater provides high levels of nutrients both in the water column and in sediment, making estuaries among the most productive natural habitats in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Rio_de_la_Plata_BA_2.JPG/330px-Rio_de_la_Plata_BA_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estuary","wikidata":"Q47053"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Mattole_River_Estuary_2005.jpg/500px-Mattole_River_Estuary_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Mattole_River_Estuary_2005.jpg/500px-Mattole_River_Estuary_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mattole River estuary","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMattole_River_Estuary_2005.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/New_York_STS058-081-038.jpg/500px-New_York_STS058-081-038.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/New_York_STS058-081-038.jpg/1280px-New_York_STS058-081-038.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/New_York_STS058-081-038.jpg/500px-New_York_STS058-081-038.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"New York–New Jersey Harbor Estuary","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANew_York_STS058-081-038.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Exe_estuary_from_balloon.jpg/500px-Exe_estuary_from_balloon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.